      "math_academy_id": "e-4713",
      "problem": "Express $4^{8} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{7}{4}\\right)^5$ as the product of two exponentiated integers.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{8}\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{4})}^{5}={4}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{5}}{{4}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n4^{8} \\cdot  \\dfrac{7^5}{4^5} =  \\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5 =4^{8-5}\\cdot 7^5=4^{3}\\cdot 7^5\n$$",
